ise  we were just very fortunate  with how it all played out so mike you  were already in pretty good shape when  you took on this challenge you just  walked across america but you say the  biggest challenge was in fact  mental how so  uh man climbing mount everest was a lot  harder  than i thought it was it was  it pushed me to all my limits um  we were there as john mentioned about a  few months  and just base camp did that about 17  500 feet and so  there's really no way to explain to  somebody who hasn't been there  uh what that does to your body you just  don't really  feel good for those entire two months  and the higher we go up just to camp two  you know i don't really sleep up there  and so  by by the just to give you an idea of  how exhausted i was  one when i got down i my body was  emitting smells i had never smelled  you know when we finally got back to  kathmandu i i fell asleep  i think for 36 out of those first 48  hours this is how  exhausted i was  mentally you know it's really hard  john's really good at that  um but you're you're at the whims of the  weather  you know there are certain days you can  climb mount everest and there's others  that it will kill you and so the weather  is  like the weather at home it's always  changing and it's imperfect  and so it is an emotional roller coaster  going from you know hey we're going to  climb today to no we're not  this is all that's part of the journey  as well well  we had some weather too you know it was  very  challenging that there were two cyclones  that came off the bay of bengal  so most seasons the 20th to the 30th you  can climb to the summit  this year we had to wait so it was even  more of a test of patience  but we had drilled that through some of  our other simulations on other mountains  all over the world and so  it was you know mike was ready he was  ready to do it well it's great to see  the two of you sitting side by side  despite the smells that were coming from  mike's body clearly 